BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:waag-trust-me-im-an-airtist-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212851Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Trust me\, I'm an AI/rtist: Open Call for Creative Interv
 entions
DESCRIPTION:Waag Futurelab invites artists\, theatre makers\, choreographer
 s\, dancers\, game designers and other creative practitioners to engage di
 rectly with seven experimental generative AI Enablers being co-created wit
 h technologists and dance\, theatre and gaming professionals. The call is 
 an invitation into the development process: selected artists get access to
  the technologies\, datasets\, interfaces and assumptions that shape these
  systems while they are still being built. Rather than testing whether the
  systems work\, artists are asked to interrogate why they work the way the
 y do\, who they are designed for\, what forms of creativity they enable or
  constrain\, and what assumptions about culture\, intelligence\, authorshi
 p\, labour\, bodies\, audiences and society are embedded in them. Waag is 
 looking for individuals and collectives willing to move beyond adoption in
 to investigation\, and welcomes proposals that are speculative\, critical\
 , poetic\, provocative\, humorous\, performative\, constructive or disrupt
 ive\, including work that challenges\, misuses\, reimagines\, hacks\, subv
 erts or plays with the tools\, exposes hidden biases\, questions environme
 ntal costs\, or imagines alternative technological futures. THE SEVEN AI E
 NABLERS\, each requiring an on-site visit to its technology partner: the G
 eneric Procedural Content Generation Enabler (text-to-3D assets and avatar
 s\, CERTH\, Greece)\; the Data-Driven Cultural Content Adaptation Enabler 
 (audience-aware creative content ideation with ranked candidate ideas\, CE
 RTH\, Greece)\; the Cross-Domain Audience Engagement Analytics Enabler (tu
 rning heterogeneous cultural participation data into interpretable engagem
 ent signals\, The Audience Agency\, Warrington\, UK)\; the Dance Style Ana
 lysis and Generation Enabler (dance video into virtual 3D choreography\, d
 issected into body\, effort\, shape and space components\, Centre of Excel
 lence for Research & Innovation\, Cyprus)\; the Immersive Design Integrati
 on Enabler for Dance & Theatre (NLP-driven XR environments with speech-to-
 3D scene editing\, Cyprus)\; the Music-to-Movement Translation Enabler (au
 dio into a time-aligned feature set spanning genre classification\, emotio
 nal valence-arousal analysis\, rhythm extraction and latent-space projecti
 on\, feeding motion generation\, spatial audio and visual choreography\, A
 ristotle University of Thessaloniki\, Greece)\; and the Adaptive Narrative
  Enabler for Games & Theatre (an evolutionary AI trained on creator-writte
 n story elements that generates infinite story variations\, explicitly rej
 ecting LLMs because of their dependence on copyrighted or public-domain co
 rpora that encode 19th and early 20th century sexism\, racism and imperial
 ism\, CAUSA Creation\, Austria). ELIGIBILITY: artists\, designers\, creati
 ve technologists\, theatre makers\, dancers\, choreographers and game desi
 gners\, as individuals or collectives\; applicants must hold a European VA
 T number. An info session with live demonstrations of the AI Enablers runs
  31 July 2026\, 10:00 to 11:30 CEST (meeting ID 995 7016 2341). Applicatio
 ns go through Homerun using the official Application Template\, uploaded a
 s a PDF\; read the Guide for Applicants\, which contains the full Enabler 
 descriptions\, before applying. Questions: zoenie at waag dot org. The AI 
 Enablers and this call are part of the Horizon Europe project HAMLET\, whi
 ch aims to make generative AI accessible to cultural organisations of all 
 sizes. Closes 30 September 2026\, 23:59 CEST. Free to apply.\n\nOrganisati
 on: Waag Futurelab (Horizon Europe project HAMLET)\nLocation: Europe. Enga
 ging with a specific AI Enabler includes an on-site visit to its technolog
 y partner: CERTH (Greece)\, The Audience Agency (Warrington\, UK)\, the Ce
 ntre of Excellence for Research & Innovation (Cyprus)\, Aristotle Universi
 ty of Thessaloniki (Greece)\, or CAUSA Creation (Austria). Final presentat
 ions September 2027.\nAward: EUR 20\,000 commission fee excluding VAT\, in
 clusive of artist fee\, travel\, accommodation and production costs. Appli
 cants must have a VAT number in Europe. No application fee.\nDuration: Jan
 uary to September 2027\, with final presentations in September 2027\n\nMor
 e: https://waag.org/en/article/open-call-creative-interventions-trust-me-i
 m-airtist/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://waag.org/en/article/open-call-creative-interventions-trust-me-i
 m-airtist/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Europe. Engaging with a specific AI Enabler includes an on-site vi
 sit to its technology partner: CERTH (Greece)\, The Audience Agency (Warri
 ngton\, UK)\, the Centre of Excellence for Research & Innovation (Cyprus)\
 , Aristotle University of Thessaloniki (Greece)\, or CAUSA Creation (Austr
 ia). Final presentations September 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:rug-lecturer-arts-culture-media-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212851Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: University of Groningen: Lecturer 4\, Arts\, Culture and 
 Media
DESCRIPTION:A one-year teaching appointment in the Faculty of Arts at the U
 niversity of Groningen\, covering courses across the BA in Arts\, Culture 
 and Media and the Popular Music and Sonic Cultures master's track. Teachin
 g spans audiovisual arts\, popular music and arts management at both under
 graduate and postgraduate level. This is a teaching-weighted post rather t
 han a research one\, so it suits practitioners who want a formal academic 
 foothold in audiovisual and sonic culture without committing to a research
  track. REQUIREMENTS: a research master's in an appropriate arts disciplin
 e\, undergraduate teaching experience\, and English at C1 (CEFR). PREFERRE
 D: a PhD in an appropriate arts discipline\, a BKO or UTQ teaching qualifi
 cation\, postgraduate teaching experience\, musicology expertise\, Dutch a
 t B2\, and familiarity with the Dutch higher education system. Apply throu
 gh the AcademicTransfer posting (vacancy ID V26.2320-EN). Closes 15 August
  2026\; appointment starts 1 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: University o
 f Groningen\, Faculty of Arts\nLocation: Groningen\, Netherlands (Broerstr
 aat 5)\nAward: EUR 3\,706 to 5\,760 gross per month at 1.0 FTE (the post i
 s 0.8 FTE)\, plus standard Dutch university holiday and year-end allowance
 s.\nDuration: Temporary\, 12-month fixed-term appointment at 0.8 FTE\, sta
 rting 1 September 2026\n\nMore: https://www.academictransfer.com/en/jobs/3
 62915/lecturer-4-arts-culture-and-media/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.academictransfer.com/en/jobs/362915/lecturer-4-arts-culture
 -and-media/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Groningen\, Netherlands (Broerstraat 5)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:uva-phd-sound-of-disagreement-nordics-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212851Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260820
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260821
SUMMARY:Deadline: University of Amsterdam: PhD\, Protest and Political Spee
 ch in the Nordics 1930-1970 (Sound of Disagreement)
DESCRIPTION:Fully funded four-year PhD position within 'The Sound of Disagr
 eement: Embodied Histories of Political Speech 1800-present'\, an interdis
 ciplinary project on how political groups have argued in public and how di
 sagreement sounds. This sub-project covers Nordic labour movements and the
  articulation of protest from roughly 1930 to 1970\, explicitly working on
  the sonic practices of political debate and the mediatization of protest\
 , so it suits researchers with a sound studies\, media studies or oral his
 tory angle as much as historians. REQUIREMENTS: a master's degree in Histo
 ry\, European Studies\, Media Studies\, Nordic Studies or a related discip
 line\; fluency in Danish\, Finnish\, Norwegian or Swedish depending on the
  chosen case study\; professional English\; archival research skills\; and
  a collaborative disposition. Apply via the AcademicTransfer posting (vaca
 ncy number 15290) with a motivation letter\, CV and research statement. De
 adline 20 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation: University of Amsterdam (UvA)\, Fa
 culty of Humanities\, Department of European Studies\nLocation: Amsterdam\
 , Netherlands (Kloveniersburgwal 48)\nAward: EUR 3\,059 to 3\,881 gross pe
 r month at 38 hours per week\, on the Dutch university PhD scale\, plus 8 
 percent holiday allowance and 8.3 percent year-end bonus. No application f
 ee.\nDuration: 48 months full-time (16-month initial contract\, extended b
 y 32 months after a positive evaluation)\; preferred start 1 January 2027\
 n\nMore: https://www.academictransfer.com/en/jobs/362349/phd-position-on-p
 rotest-and-political-speech-in-the-nordics-1930-1970/?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.academictransfer.com/en/jobs/362349/phd-position-on-protest
 -and-political-speech-in-the-nordics-1930-1970/?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Amsterdam\, Netherlands (Kloveniersburgwal 48)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:glina3-open-call-type-language-visual-systems-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212851Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260812
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260813
SUMMARY:Deadline: GLINA3: Open Call for a Collaborator Working with Type\, 
 Language and Visual Systems
DESCRIPTION:GLINA3 is an Amsterdam clay 3D printing and digital design stud
 io\, and it is looking for one collaborator to join a new research project
  at the intersection of typography\, language\, technology and material cu
 lture. They want an artist\, writer\, poet\, anthropologist or artistic re
 searcher whose existing practice already engages with type design\, typogr
 aphy\, writing systems\, visual language\, fonts and letterforms\, languag
 e technologies\, or the tools that shape how language is produced\, read o
 r translated. This is an unusually good fit for anyone working on the mach
 ine-readable side of language: the brief explicitly names language technol
 ogies and translation tooling alongside classical type design. ELIGIBILITY
 : individual practitioners with demonstrable work in one or more of the li
 sted areas. HOW TO APPLY: the studio asks for relevant work\, research\, p
 ublications or projects connected to type\, fonts\, writing systems\, visu
 al language or the tools that shape them\, and nothing else. Because the d
 eadline is tight they say Instagram DM is the fastest route\; you can also
  email a short introduction and one relevant project to office@glina3.nl. 
 NOTE: the fee is confirmed only if the project's own funding comes through
 \, so clarify terms before committing time.\n\nOrganisation: GLINA3\nLocat
 ion: Amsterdam\, Netherlands (studio is Amsterdam-based\; collaboration te
 rms not specified)\nAward: Paid collaboration. The studio states the fee w
 ill be confirmed upon successful funding\, so the amount is contingent and
  not published up front. No application fee.\nDuration: New collaborative 
 research project\, length not specified\n\nMore: https://www.instagram.com
 /p/Dbse-4jDNj5/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.instagram.com/p/Dbse-4jDNj5/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Amsterdam\, Netherlands (studio is Amsterdam-based\; collaboration
  terms not specified)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:nordic-currents-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212851Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: Nordic Currents 2027-2028 Open Call (Nordic Council of Mi
 nisters)
DESCRIPTION:Nordic Currents is a new international culture initiative from 
 the Nordic Council of Ministers\, running 2027-2028\, that invites cultura
 l organisations in European countries neighbouring the Nordic region to jo
 in forces with Nordic partners on projects that move audiences\, deepen re
 lationships and create lasting connections. Trust\, openness\, artistic fr
 eedom and democratic values are named as its core. The stated aims are to 
 showcase the richness and quality of contemporary Nordic culture and art i
 n neighbouring European countries and to build long-term collaborations be
 tween Nordic and European actors. WHO CAN APPLY: established organisations
  based in European countries neighbouring the Nordic region\, primarily Be
 lgium\, Estonia\, France\, Germany\, Ireland\, Lithuania\, Latvia\, the Ne
 therlands\, Poland and the United Kingdom. Eligible applicant types are cu
 ltural organisations\, institutions\, promoters\, festivals and producers\
 , as well as embassies and diplomatic missions working in cooperation with
  arts and culture stakeholders. Every application must have at least two p
 artners from different Nordic countries who stay active throughout the pro
 ject. WHERE: in a single country or as a cooperation across several countr
 ies\, but activities must primarily take place in the country where the ap
 plying organisation is based\; if several European partners outside the No
 rdic region are involved\, activities may also be organised in their count
 ries. Emphasis goes to projects in regions and countries with existing loc
 al interest in Nordic art and culture\, where Nordic and local partners ca
 n build long-term\, mutually beneficial collaborations. WHAT THE PROJECT M
 UST INCLUDE: both artistic programmes and events accessible to local audie
 nces\, and platforms for professional exchange and sector development. Pub
 lic cultural events can be festivals\, programmes\, series or other curate
 d formats\, standalone or attached to existing structures. Projects must a
 lso include network-building\, knowledge sharing and other forms of sector
  development. Programming must include representation from all the Nordic 
 countries\, with artists and cultural actors from Denmark\, the Faroe Isla
 nds\, Finland\, Greenland (Kalaallit Nunaat)\, Iceland\, Norway\, Sweden a
 nd Aland. Projects must take place between 1 June 2027 and 1 June 2028. FU
 NDING: applications may request EUR 100\,000 to EUR 800\,000\; the total p
 ool is EUR 800\,000\, so between one and eight proposals will be successfu
 l. Co-financing through local\, national or other funding sources is a pre
 requisite for eligibility. HOW TO APPLY: proposals are submitted in Englis
 h through an online portal open only between 1 October and 1 November 2026
 . ASSESSMENT: all eligible proposals go to a professional committee and ex
 pert reviewers in the corresponding art fields\, judged on artistic qualit
 y\, connection to the Nordic region\, relevance to the artistic fields cov
 ered\, how the concept incorporates democratic values such as openness\, t
 rust and artistic freedom\, capacity to implement the collaboration\, publ
 ic appeal for the intended audiences\, public accessibility\, potential fo
 r long-term impact and future collaboration\, and organisational and finan
 cial capacity. Selected projects are publicly announced in May 2027.\n\nOr
 ganisation: Nordic Council of Ministers\nLocation: Projects take place pri
 marily in the applicant organisation's home country\, in European countrie
 s neighbouring the Nordic region: primarily Belgium\, Estonia\, France\, G
 ermany\, Ireland\, Lithuania\, Latvia\, the Netherlands\, Poland and the U
 nited Kingdom.\nAward: EUR 100\,000 to EUR 800\,000 per project. The total
  pool for the initiative is EUR 800\,000\, so between one and eight propos
 als will be funded. Co-financing from local\, national or other sources is
  a prerequisite for eligibility. No application fee.\nDuration: Projects m
 ust take place between 1 June 2027 and 1 June 2028\n\nMore: https://www.no
 rden.org/en/nordiccurrents?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_mediu
 m=referral
URL:https://www.norden.org/en/nordiccurrents?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Projects take place primarily in the applicant organisation's home
  country\, in European countries neighbouring the Nordic region: primarily
  Belgium\, Estonia\, France\, Germany\, Ireland\, Lithuania\, Latvia\, the
  Netherlands\, Poland and the United Kingdom.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iffr-darkroom-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212851Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Darkroom 2027: Work-in-Progress Showcase (IFFR Pro)
DESCRIPTION:Darkroom is IFFR's work-in-progress platform\, running during C
 ineMart\, where recently completed or nearly finished films are launched t
 o the international film industry. Selected teams present an excerpt of th
 eir work to international sales agents\, festival programmers\, buyers and
  other industry professionals in a closed presentation\, and receive tailo
 red mentorship matched to the project's needs from senior editors\, market
 ing strategists\, festival experts and distribution professionals. All Dar
 kroom projects get a curated agenda of one-to-one meetings with potential 
 creative and business partners plus facilitated informal networking\, and 
 IFFR Pro awards are handed out to projects from the selection. ELIGIBILITY
 : the project must have been supported by IFFR through at least one of the
  following: new for 2027\, the filmmaker has been selected for the Tiger C
 ompetition or Bright Future programme at IFFR within the last 10 years\; t
 he project has been supported by the Hubert Bals Fund through any of its f
 unding schemes\; the project was formerly presented at BoostNL\; the proje
 ct was formerly presented at CineMart\; or the project's main producer or 
 main co-producer has been a Rotterdam Lab participant. In addition\, the p
 roject must have no sales agent attached\, Darkroom must be its first work
 -in-progress showcase\, and it must not have a confirmed world or internat
 ional premiere at the time of application (if that changes after selection
  at a winter festival\, applicants must tell the Darkroom team immediately
  so eligibility can be rechecked). Projects that do not meet all criteria 
 are ineligible. Recent editions selected 6 to 12 projects. Deadline 1 Octo
 ber 2026\, 22:00 UTC. Free to submit.\n\nOrganisation: International Film 
 Festival Rotterdam (IFFR)\nLocation: Rotterdam\, Netherlands. Presentation
 s take place during CineMart at IFFR 2027 (28 January to 7 February 2027).
  Open internationally: eligibility is defined by the project's prior conne
 ction to IFFR\, not by the applicant's country.\nAward: No participation f
 ee and no fee to submit. Selected projects compete for the IFFR Pro awards
  attached to Darkroom: the Eurimages Outreach Award of EUR 30\,000 (shared
  with Lightroom)\, the HBF Empowerment Award of EUR 10\,000\, the Filmmore
  Work-in-progress Post-production Award of EUR 5\,000\, and the 4DR Studio
 s Award (an in-kind studio or workshop prize\, shared with CineMart). Part
 icipation also includes tailored mentorship\, a closed industry presentati
 on and a curated agenda of one-to-one meetings. Award line-up is per the m
 ost recent published edition and may change for 2027.\nDuration: Work-in-p
 rogress showcase during CineMart at IFFR 2027\, 28 January to 7 February 2
 027\n\nMore: https://iffr.com/en/iffr-pro/darkroom?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://iffr.com/en/iffr-pro/darkroom?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Rotterdam\, Netherlands. Presentations take place during CineMart 
 at IFFR 2027 (28 January to 7 February 2027). Open internationally: eligib
 ility is defined by the project's prior connection to IFFR\, not by the ap
 plicant's country.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:filmfonds-netherlands-film-production-incentive-2026@artificialnouveau.
 github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212851Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261026
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261027
SUMMARY:Deadline: Netherlands Film Production Incentive (October 2026 round
 )
DESCRIPTION:The Netherlands Film Production Incentive is the Film Fund's au
 tomatic cash rebate for feature films\, animated films and documentaries\,
  returning up to 35 percent of qualifying Dutch production costs. It is de
 signed to keep production spend in the Netherlands and is assessed on a po
 ints system rather than artistic panel review. WHO CAN APPLY: only Dutch p
 roducers representing independent production companies. The company must b
 e legally registered in the Netherlands (including Bonaire\, Sint Eustatiu
 s and Saba) for at least two years\, operate continuously in film or audio
 visual production\, and be represented by a producer who has produced at l
 east one majority film with a budget of EUR 500\,000 or more that had a Du
 tch cinema release. PROJECT THRESHOLDS: feature films and animated films n
 eed a minimum production budget of EUR 1 million and at least EUR 150\,000
  in qualifying costs\; documentaries need a minimum budget of EUR 250\,000
  and at least EUR 100\,000 in qualifying costs. FURTHER CONDITIONS: a guar
 anteed Dutch cinema release with a minimum 12-week window\, at least 50 pe
 rcent of the budget unconditionally promised in writing by third parties\,
  at least two unaffiliated financiers\, and a minimum score of 75 points o
 n the automatic assessment system. Deadline 26 October 2026 at 17:00 CET.\
 n\nOrganisation: Netherlands Film Fund (Nederlands Filmfonds)\nLocation: N
 etherlands. Applicant production companies must be legally registered in t
 he Netherlands\, including Bonaire\, Sint Eustatius and Saba.\nAward: Cash
  rebate of up to 35 percent of qualifying Dutch production costs\, capped 
 at EUR 3 million per year per applicant production company. The annual bud
 get is EUR 20 million divided across four rounds at roughly EUR 5 million 
 per round\, with an extra EUR 3 million in the first round. No application
  fee.\nDuration: Per-project production incentive\, applied for per round\
 n\nMore: https://www.filmfonds.nl/en/funding/fund/netherlands-film-product
 ion-incentive?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.filmfonds.nl/en/funding/fund/netherlands-film-production-in
 centive?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Netherlands. Applicant production companies must be legally regist
 ered in the Netherlands\, including Bonaire\, Sint Eustatius and Saba.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:filmfonds-production-incentive-high-end-series-2026@artificialnouveau.g
 ithub.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212851Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261026
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261027
SUMMARY:Deadline: Netherlands Film Production Incentive: High-End Series (O
 ctober 2026 round)
DESCRIPTION:The High-End Series strand of the Netherlands Film Production I
 ncentive extends the Film Fund's 35 percent cash rebate to high-end drama\
 , documentary and animated television series\, returning up to 35 percent 
 of qualifying Dutch production costs. WHO CAN APPLY: independent productio
 n companies based for at least two years in the Netherlands\, an EU member
  state\, the EEA or Switzerland\, producing audiovisual content on a conti
 nuing basis\, and represented by a producer who has produced either a majo
 rity film with a minimum budget of EUR 500\,000 and a Dutch cinema release
 \, or a majority television series in the same category. Note that despite
  the broader basing rule\, only applicants from the Netherlands\, Bonaire\
 , Sint Eustatius and Saba may actually apply. PROJECT THRESHOLDS: minimum 
 production budget per broadcast minute varies by category\, at EUR 4\,000 
 for documentary and EUR 8\,000 to EUR 12\,000 for drama and animation. FUR
 THER CONDITIONS: at least 50 percent of the production budget secured in w
 riting from third parties\, a guaranteed television or VOD broadcast in th
 e Netherlands (or in two or more countries for co-productions)\, and a min
 imum score of 75 points on the automatic assessment system. Deadline 26 Oc
 tober 2026 at 17:00 CET.\n\nOrganisation: Netherlands Film Fund (Nederland
 s Filmfonds)\nLocation: Netherlands. Only applicants from the Netherlands\
 , Bonaire\, Sint Eustatius and Saba may apply.\nAward: Cash rebate of up t
 o 35 percent of qualifying Dutch production costs\, capped at EUR 3 millio
 n per project per year per applicant production company. The 2026 annual b
 udget is EUR 9.5 million across three rounds\, plus an additional EUR 0.4 
 million in the first round carried over from 2025. No application fee.\nDu
 ration: Per-project production incentive\, applied for per round\n\nMore: 
 https://www.filmfonds.nl/en/funding/fund/netherlands-film-production-incen
 tive-high-end-series?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=refe
 rral
URL:https://www.filmfonds.nl/en/funding/fund/netherlands-film-production-in
 centive-high-end-series?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
LOCATION:Netherlands. Only applicants from the Netherlands\, Bonaire\, Sint
  Eustatius and Saba may apply.
